# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 has "unite_appearance → fare_well" and "unite_appearance"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 have "unite_appearance → fare_well" and "(unite_appearance → fare_well) → ¬principle_for_justice"; they differ when only "we unite the worst injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances", "we shall fare to our mind both with gods and men, in life and after death" and "there is a principle on which we should any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice" are true, which the first allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "we unite the worst injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances", "we shall fare to our mind both with gods and men, in life and after death" and "there is a principle on which we should any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice" are true. Is S1, "On what principle, then, shall we any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice? when, if we only unite the latter with a deceitful regard to appearances, we shall fare to our mind both with gods and men, in life and after death, as the most numerous and the highest authorities tell us.", true then?
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 have nothing from it; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 has "¬principle_for_justice → ¬superior_honours_justice"; they differ when only "a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth is willing to honour justice" is true, which the first allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth is willing to honour justice" is true and "there is a principle on which we should any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice" is false. Is S2, "Knowing all this, Socrates, how can a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth, be willing to honour justice; or indeed to refrain from laughing when he hears justice praised?", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 has "unjust_when_powerful"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 have "unjust_when_powerful" and "unjust_when_powerful → blames_only_if_powerless"; they differ when only "when he obtains the power, he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be" is true, which the first allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "when he obtains the power, he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be" is true and "he only blames injustice who, owing to cowardice or age or some weakness, has not the power of being unjust" is false. Is S5, "And this is proved by the fact that when he obtains the power, he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
plato-republic-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 has "fare_well → ¬principle_for_justice" and "¬principle_for_justice → ¬superior_honours_justice" and "unjust_when_powerful → blames_only_if_powerless"; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.2 has "¬principle_for_justice → ¬superior_honours_justice"; they differ when only "we shall fare to our mind both with gods and men, in life and after death" and "there is a principle on which we should any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice" are true, which the second allows; plato-republic-1.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/negation: plato-republic-1.logic.r1 C (S2): the sentence states "a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth is willing to honour justice", with no word of denial, but C denies it: it reads "it is not so that a man who has any superiority of mind or person or rank or wealth is willing to honour justice" [in 1 of 2 runs]
